      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to use the Pythagorean theorem. The Pythagorean theorem states that in a right triangle, the square of the length of the hypotenuse (the side opposite the right angle) is equal to the sum of the squares of the lengths of the other two sides. The formula is:\n\n\\[ c^2 = a^2 + b^2 \\]\n\nwhere \\( c \\) is the length of the hypotenuse, and \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the lengths of the other two sides.\n\nIn this triangle, we know two of the sides:\n- One side is 6 cm.\n- Another side is 8 cm.\n- One angle is a right angle, which means the longest side (hypotenuse) is opposite the right angle.\n\nLet's call the remaining side \\( x \\), where \\( x \\) could be the hypotenuse or one of the legs. We need to find the shortest possible length of the remaining side. There are two scenarios to consider:\n\n1. \\( x \\) is the hypotenuse.\n2. \\( x \\) is one of the legs.\n\n**Scenario 1: \\( x \\) is the hypotenuse.**\n\nHere, we use the Pythagorean theorem:\n\n\\[ x^2 = 6^2 + 8^2 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 36 + 64 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 100 \\]\n\\[ x = \\sqrt{100} \\]\n\\[ x = 10 \\]\n\nSo, if \\( x \\) is the hypotenuse, the length of the remaining side is 10 cm.\n\n**Scenario 2: \\( x \\) is one of the legs.**\n\nHere, we use the Pythagorean theorem but rearrange it to solve for the hypotenuse:\n\n\\[ x^2 = 6^2 - 8^2 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = 36 - 64 \\]\n\\[ x^2 = -28 \\]\n\nThis results in a negative value under the square root, which is not possible since the length of a side cannot be negative. Therefore, \\( x \\) cannot be a leg of the triangle if the other two sides are 6 cm and 8 cm.\n\nThus, the only possible length for the remaining side is when it is the hypotenuse, which is 10 cm.\n\nThe shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10.00}\n\\]",
